23:28:17 | INFO | 🤖 Target initial answer: The Concorde and supersonic commercial flight were never officially “abolished” by a governing body, but they were retired for a combination of economic, technical, and environmental reasons that made them unsustainable. The Concorde’s last flight was in 2003, and since then no airline has operated a supersonic passenger service. Here’s why: ### 1. **Crippling Operating Costs** - **Fuel consumption:** The Concorde burned about 25,000 liters of fuel per hour, roughly 2–3 times more per passenger than a subsonic jet like the Boeing 747. After the 1973 oil crisis and again in the 2000s, high fuel prices made each flight enormously expensive. - **Maintenance:** The small fleet (only 20 were built, 14 used commercially) meant spare parts were scarce and bespoke. Aging airframes required ever more intensive—and costly—maintenance. - **Limited capacity:** With only 100 seats (all first-class), the Concorde couldn’t benefit from economies of scale. Tickets were priced at $10,000+ round-trip, yet even full planes often lost money. ### 2. **Sonic Boom and Route Restrictions** - A supersonic aircraft generates a continuous sonic boom that can shatter windows and disturb communities on the ground. This led to a near-total ban on supersonic flight over land. - As a result, the Concorde could only fly over water, limiting it to transatlantic routes (London/Paris to New York/Washington). It could never tap into lucrative overland markets like Paris–Moscow or London–Tokyo without subsonic detours that erased its speed advantage. ### 3. **Noise and Environmental Concerns** - In addition to the sonic boom, the Concorde was extremely loud on takeoff and landing, far exceeding modern noise limits at airports. Airport curfews further restricted its schedule. - Growing environmental awareness in the 1990s and 2000s put pressure on airlines to reduce emissions. The Concorde’s per-passenger carbon footprint was much higher than that of subsonic aircraft. ### 4. **The Crash of Air France Flight 4590 (2000)** - The July 2000 accident near Paris killed all 109 people on board and four on the ground. It was the Concorde’s only fatal incident, but it shattered the aircraft’s reputation for safety. All Concordes were grounded for over a year while modifications were made. When they returned, passenger confidence had slumped—just as the airline industry was hit by the post‑9/11 downturn. ### 5. **Shifting Market Dynamics** - Concorde’s original business case assumed that speed‑obsessed business travellers would pay any price. By the 1990s, high‑end business class on subsonic jets offered lie‑flat beds, satellite phones, and much lower fares, eroding Concorde’s only advantage. - Airbus (which took over the support role) announced in 2003 that it would stop supplying maintenance. Both British Airways and Air France concluded it was no longer economically viable to keep flying a 30‑year‑old fleet. ### Was it “abolished completely”? Not exactly. The Concorde was retired voluntarily by its operators. Supersonic flight itself wasn’t banned—only the rules on overland sonic booms remain in place. Several companies (e.g., Boom Supersonic’s Overture, NASA’s X‑59 Quesst) are now working on a new generation of lower‑boom, more efficient supersonic airliners. But as of today, the commercial supersonic era remains on hold because the original technology could not be economically or environmentally sustained.
